Subject: [PATCH 20/20] libopenbios: the Forth bindings report their own failures
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

Four defects in this lab shipped behind ONE silence, and this is that silence.

feval() has ALWAYS returned the throw code -- eword() wraps the call in `catch`
and hands the result back. Counted in this tree at e5ac46d:

    146  feval() call sites,  of which  1  looks at the return value
    969  fword() call sites,  of which  0  do

The one is packages/cmdline.c:257, the interactive command line, which pushes
the code straight into print-status. Everywhere else, a Forth fragment that
threw -- or a word that was not in the dictionary at all -- executed nothing,
said nothing, and the C caller carried on as though it had worked.

What that cost, in this lab alone:

  * fword("eval2") in libopenbios/initprogram.c named a word DEFINED NOWHERE IN
    THE TREE. findword() returned 0, _fword() returned -1, the macro discarded
    it, and OpenBIOS's Forth-source loader silently evaluated nothing. (patch 15)
  * feval("['] vga-driver-fcode 2 cells + 1 byte-load") in drivers/pci.c threw
    on x86 for years: the VGA FCode driver had never run. (patch 18)
  * openbios_init()'s bind_func()s landed in an open device node, so
    (init-program) was unreachable and `load` failed with a message from a
    DIFFERENT layer -- which is where the search then started. (patches 14, 16)
  * amd64's preopen left /chosen active, so words defined at the prompt
    evaporated. (patch 19)

All four are one failure -- "the word is not where the caller is looking" --
and all four were invisible here.

WHY THE OLD OUTPUT WAS WORSE THAN NOTHING. For an undefined word the
interpreter does print something: forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs:64 does
`type 3a emit`, the offending word then a colon, and throws. With no status
printed by anyone, the whole failure is

    vga-driver-fcode:

no newline, so the NEXT printk lands on the same line and the pair reads as a
progress marker. This lab believed exactly that for long enough to write the
string into drivers/floppy.c as a boot landmark. The new line COMPLETES that
half-message rather than competing with it:

    vga-driver-fcode:feval: ['] vga-driver-fcode 2 cells + 1 byte-load -- threw
    -19 (hex -13; the Forth sources spell throw codes in HEX -- see
    print-status, forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs)

THREE THINGS THIS PATCH GOT WRONG FIRST, each caught by measuring:

1. `%.96s` PRINTED A MANGLED HALF-LINE. libc/vsprintf.c's %s case has the
   correct `strnlen(s, precision)` under an `#if 0`, and the live branch is

       len = mstrlen(s);
       if( precision > len ) len = precision;

   -- precision is a MINIMUM, not a maximum, so `%.96s` on a 13-byte string
   prints 96 bytes, 83 of them past the terminator. A diagnostic that
   over-reads to report an error is not a diagnostic. Clipped in C instead.
   The vsprintf bug is REPORTED, NOT FIXED here: different subsystem, needs its
   own fixtures, and fs/hfsplus/hfsp_record.c's two `%4.4s` are its only other
   callers (both of which can currently print MORE than four characters).

2. REPORTING FROM feval() ON THE INTERACTIVE PATH IS NOISE. First draft:

       0 > pwd
       no active devicefeval: pwd -- threw -2 (...)
        Aborted.

   -- the same failure said twice, once by a layer with no business saying it.
   A channel that fires on ordinary typos is one people learn to ignore, which
   recreates the silence being fixed. feval_quiet() exists for the one caller
   that renders the status itself; cmdline.c uses it.

3. THE CODE IS -19, NOT -13, AND BOTH ARE RIGHT. OpenBIOS's Forth runs in base
   16, so the `-13 throw` in interpreter.fs is hex -- decimal -19, which is why
   kernel/bootstrap.c's table says `case -19:` for "undefined word." while the
   Forth table says -13. The message prints both; the first draft of the smoke
   assertion looked for -13 and went red against a working reporter. No C copy
   of the code->name table is made: this file's own history says copies drift,
   so the message names where the table lives instead.

TESTED, and the track is two-sided in a single boot on all three arches
(`./smoke-openbios.sh diagnostics`):

  (1) a clean boot prints ZERO feval:/fword:/eword: lines -- x86, amd64, ppc
  (2) test-feval-report, bound in libopenbios/init.c so one definition serves
      every arch, prints EXACTLY ONE, naming a word that cannot exist

Neither half is worth having alone: (1) reads identically whether the reporter
works or was compiled out, and (2) would pass a reporter that fires on
everything.

NEGATIVE CONTROL, run not reasoned. Patch 18's defect re-injected (device-end
removed from arch/amd64/init.fs) alongside a bogus fword("no-such-word-control")
in openbios_init(). Both branches printed and assertion (1) bit -- and the
control immediately found a flaw in the assertion itself: it matched
'^(feval|fword|eword):' and reported 1 failure where there were 2, because the
undefined-word case CONTINUES interpreter.fs's line and never starts one. A
line-anchored regex standing in for a question about a message, which is the
mistake tools/check-harness-net.sh made twice. Unanchored, it reports both.

NOT WATCHED TO FIRE: _eword()'s "word not in the dictionary" branch. It is
reachable only if `evaluate` itself is missing, which cannot happen in a
firmware that has reached the prompt. Named rather than counted as covered.

diff --git a/include/libopenbios/bindings.h b/include/libopenbios/bindings.h
--- a/include/libopenbios/bindings.h
+++ b/include/libopenbios/bindings.h
@@ -88,6 +88,9 @@ extern char		*pop_fstr_copy( void );
 
 extern int		_fword( const char *word, xt_t *cache_xt );
 extern cell		_eword( const char *word, xt_t *cache_xt, int nargs );
+/* feval() reports a throw; feval_quiet() does not. Use the quiet one ONLY when
+ * the caller renders the status itself -- see libopenbios/bindings.c. */
+extern cell		feval_quiet( const char *str );
 extern int		_selfword( const char *method, xt_t *cache_xt );
 extern int		_parword( const char *method, xt_t *cache_xt );
 
diff --git a/libopenbios/bindings.c b/libopenbios/bindings.c
--- a/libopenbios/bindings.c
+++ b/libopenbios/bindings.c
@@ -32,14 +32,143 @@ push_str( const char *str )
 	PUSH( str ? strlen(str) : 0 );
 }
 
-/* WARNING: sloooow - AVOID */
+/* THE BINDINGS REPORT THEIR OWN FAILURES, and until 2026-08-26 they did not.
+ *
+ * feval() has ALWAYS returned the throw code -- eword() wraps the call in
+ * `catch` and hands the result back. Counted in this tree: 146 feval() call
+ * sites, of which exactly ONE looks at the return value, and 969 fword() call
+ * sites, of which NONE do. (The one is packages/cmdline.c:257, the interactive
+ * command line, which is why feval_quiet() exists below. drivers/obio.c's two
+ * `if (i != 0) fword("encode+")` are loop guards, not return checks.) So a
+ * Forth fragment that threw, or a word that was not in the dictionary at all,
+ * executed nothing and said nothing, and the C caller carried on as though it
+ * had worked.
+ *
+ * Three defects shipped behind that silence, each costing a debugging session:
+ *
+ *   - `fword("eval2")` in libopenbios/initprogram.c named a word DEFINED
+ *     NOWHERE IN THE TREE. findword() returned 0, _fword() returned -1, the
+ *     macro discarded it, and the Forth loader silently evaluated nothing.
+ *   - `feval("['] vga-driver-fcode 2 cells + 1 byte-load")` in drivers/pci.c
+ *     threw -13 on x86 for years: the VGA FCode driver had never run.
+ *   - `openbios_init()`'s bind_func()s landed in an open device node, so
+ *     `(init-program)` was unreachable and every `load` ended in a message
+ *     from a DIFFERENT layer, which is where the search then started.
+ *
+ * All three are the same failure -- "the word is not where the caller is
+ * looking" -- and all three were invisible here.
+ *
+ * WHY THE OLD OUTPUT WAS WORSE THAN NOTHING. For a -13 the interpreter does
+ * print something: forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs:64 does `type 3a emit`, the
+ * offending word followed by a colon, and then throws. With no status printed
+ * by anyone, the whole failure is
+ *
+ *     vga-driver-fcode:
+ *
+ * with no newline, so the NEXT printk lands on the same line and the pair reads
+ * as a progress marker. This lab believed exactly that for long enough to write
+ * the string into drivers/floppy.c as a boot landmark. The line below completes
+ * that half-message instead of adding a competing one.
+ *
+ * THE CODE IS PRINTED AS A NUMBER, deliberately. forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs
+ * (print-status) already holds the code->name table; a second copy in C is a
+ * copy, and this file's own history says copies drift. The message names where
+ * the table lives instead.
+ */
+static void
+report_forth_failure( const char *what, const char *subject, cell code )
+{
+	char clip[97];
+	int n = 0;
+	int c;
+
+	/* Long fragments get clipped: some feval() strings are whole colon
+	 * definitions, and a diagnostic that wraps the console is one nobody
+	 * reads. The head is the useful part -- it contains the verb.
+	 *
+	 * CLIPPED HERE, IN C, AND NOT WITH `%.96s` -- which is what the first
+	 * draft did, and which printed a mangled half-line. libc/vsprintf.c's
+	 * %s case has the correct `strnlen(s, precision)` sitting under an
+	 * `#if 0`, and the live branch is
+	 *
+	 *     len = mstrlen(s);
+	 *     if( precision > len ) len = precision;
+	 *
+	 * so precision is a MINIMUM, not a maximum: `%.96s` on a 13-byte string
+	 * prints 96 bytes, walking 83 past the terminator. A diagnostic that
+	 * over-reads to report an error is not a diagnostic. Recorded in TODO
+	 * rather than fixed here -- it is a different subsystem and needs its
+	 * own fixtures, and fs/hfsplus/hfsp_record.c's two `%4.4s` are its only
+	 * other callers. */
+	while( n < (int)sizeof(clip) - 1 && subject[n] ) {
+		clip[n] = subject[n];
+		n++;
+	}
+	clip[n] = '\0';
+
+	/* (int), not a cell-width format. FMT_CELL_d lives in kernel/cross.h,
+	 * which is a HOST header for the bootstrap tool and has no business in
+	 * target code -- and a throw code is a small integer on every arch, so
+	 * there is no width question to get wrong. This file's neighbourhood
+	 * already lost a day to `unsigned long` meaning 4 bytes on i386 and 8
+	 * on LP64 (struct e820entry, Spike 3); the narrowing here is explicit
+	 * rather than assumed. */
+	/* BOTH BASES, and the reason is a trap this message walked into on its
+	 * first run. An undefined word inside `evaluate` reports here as
+	 *
+	 *     threw -19
+	 *
+	 * while forth/bootstrap/{interpreter,bootstrap}.fs spell that same code
+	 * `-13`. They are the same number: OpenBIOS's Forth runs in BASE 16, so
+	 * the source literal -13 is hex, i.e. -19 decimal -- which is exactly
+	 * why kernel/bootstrap.c's table says `case -19:` for "undefined word."
+	 * while the Forth table says -13. Printing one base only sends the
+	 * reader looking for a code that is not in the file they open. */
+	c = (int)code;
+	printk("%s: %s%s -- threw %d (hex %s%x; the Forth sources spell throw"
+	       " codes in HEX -- see print-status,"
+	       " forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs)\n",
+	       what, clip, subject[n] ? "..." : "", c,
+	       c < 0 ? "-" : "", (unsigned)(c < 0 ? -c : c));
+}
+
+/* For the ONE caller in the tree that renders the status itself.
+ *
+ * packages/cmdline.c:257 is the interactive command line:
+ *
+ *     PUSH( feval(buf) );
+ *     fword("print-status");
+ *
+ * -- it hands the throw code straight to forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs's
+ * print-status, which is exactly right. Reporting from feval() as well would
+ * put a C diagnostic under every mistyped word at the `0 >` prompt, and a
+ * channel that fires on ordinary typos is one people learn to ignore, which
+ * recreates the silence this file is fixing. Measured before the split:
+ *
+ *     0 > pwd
+ *     no active devicefeval: pwd -- threw -2 (...)
+ *      Aborted.
+ *
+ * -- the same failure said twice, once by a layer that had no business saying
+ * it. WARNING: sloooow - AVOID (as feval). */
 cell
-feval( const char *str )
+feval_quiet( const char *str )
 {
 	push_str( str );
 	return eword("evaluate", 2);
 }
 
+/* WARNING: sloooow - AVOID */
+cell
+feval( const char *str )
+{
+	cell ret = feval_quiet( str );
+
+	if( ret )
+		report_forth_failure( "feval", str, ret );
+	return ret;
+}
+
 cell
 _eword( const char *word, xt_t *cache_xt, int nargs )
 {
@@ -56,6 +185,12 @@ _eword( const char *word, xt_t *cache_xt, int nargs )
 		enterforth( catch_xt );
 		if( (ret=POP()) )
 			dstackcnt -= nargs;
+	} else {
+		/* NOT the same thing as a -1 throw, and the two were
+		 * indistinguishable before: both left ret == -1. This branch
+		 * means the word was never found and NOTHING RAN. */
+		printk("eword: '%s' is not in the dictionary -- nothing was"
+		       " executed\n", word);
 	}
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -71,6 +206,11 @@ _fword( const char *word, xt_t *cache_xt )
 		enterforth( *cache_xt );
 		return 0;
 	}
+	/* The `eval2` shape. Only the built-in dictionary is searched, so a word
+	 * that exists as a NODE METHOD lands here too -- which is precisely the
+	 * defect patches 14, 16, 18 and 19 fixed four times over. */
+	printk("fword: '%s' is not in the dictionary -- nothing was executed\n",
+	       word);
 	return -1;
 }
 
diff --git a/libopenbios/init.c b/libopenbios/init.c
--- a/libopenbios/init.c
+++ b/libopenbios/init.c
@@ -65,6 +65,28 @@ static void lelfetch(void)
 	PUSH(__le32_to_cpu(*aaddr));
 }
 
+/* THE REPORTER'S OWN MUST-CATCH FIXTURE, bound into the firmware on purpose.
+ *
+ * "a clean boot prints no diagnostics" is a ONE-SIDED assertion: it reads
+ * exactly the same whether the reporter in libopenbios/bindings.c works or was
+ * compiled out, and a scan that matches nothing prints the same green tick as
+ * one that is broken. This word makes the pair testable in a single boot --
+ * the boot region must be silent, and this must produce exactly one feval:
+ * line naming a word that cannot exist.
+ *
+ * It is bound HERE, in libopenbios/init.c, so every arch gets it from one
+ * place rather than three copies drifting apart.
+ *
+ * Leaves the stack as it found it: the diagnostic line IS the result, and a
+ * self-test that unbalances the stack makes every probe after it read oddly.
+ * The code it provokes is "undefined word" -- -19 decimal, which the Forth
+ * sources spell -13 because they are in base 16.
+ */
+static void feval_report_selftest( void )
+{
+	(void)feval("openbios-feval-selftest-no-such-word");
+}
+
 void
 openbios_init( void )
 {
@@ -83,6 +105,9 @@ openbios_init( void )
 	// Bind the C implementation of (go) into Forth
 	bind_func("(go)", go);
 	
+	// The reporter's must-catch fixture -- see feval_report_selftest above
+	bind_func("test-feval-report", feval_report_selftest);
+
 	// Bind the LE access words
 	bind_func("le-w!", lewstore);
 	bind_func("le-l!", lelstore);
diff --git a/packages/cmdline.c b/packages/cmdline.c
--- a/packages/cmdline.c
+++ b/packages/cmdline.c
@@ -254,7 +254,9 @@ cmdline_prompt( cmdline_info_t *ci )
 
 			emit_str( &buf[pos] );
 			emit(' ');
-			PUSH( feval(buf) );
+			/* feval_quiet, because print-status on the very next
+			 * line IS the report. See libopenbios/bindings.c. */
+			PUSH( feval_quiet(buf) );
 			fword("print-status");
 
 			/* Leave the interpreter if terminate? value set */
